Whenever a Director determines that a violation of the Municipal Code or applicable state codes exists, the Director or Enforcement Official may issue a Notice of Violation to a Responsible Person. The Notice of Violation shall include the following information:

(a) The name of the property’s record owner;

(b) Street address;

(c) The code sections in violation;

(d) A description of the property’s condition which violates the applicable codes;

(e) A list of necessary corrections to bring the property into compliance;

(f) A deadline or specific date to correct the violations listed in the Notice of Violation;

(g) Reference to the potential consequences should the property remain in violation after the expiration of the compliance deadline including, but not limited to: criminal prosecution, civil injunction, administrative abatement, civil penalties, revocation of permits, recordation of the Notice of Violation and withholding of future municipal permits.

(*“Notice of Violation” added (portions previously contained in former Sec. 44.0122) 8–10–1993 by O–17956 N.S.*)

Except as otherwise provided in the City Charter, the Council shall sell the real property of the City in compliance with the requirements herein established. No real property belonging to the City shall be sold except in pursuance of a resolution passed by an affirmative vote of five members of the Council, which shall contain the following:

(a) The reason for selling such real property;

(b) A description of the real property to be sold;

(c) A statement of the value of such property as disclosed by an appraisal made by a qualified real estate appraiser, who may be a professional appraiser or a qualified employee of the City of San Diego, together with the minimum amount the Council will consider for the sale of each parcel of property;

(d) A statement that the City may at its discretion pay a real estate broker’s commission under the provisions of Section 22.0905 for the sale of such real property;

(e) A statement that the property will be sold by negotiation or by public auction, or by sealed bids, or by a combination of public auction, and sealed bids; providing, however, that in the event that such property is to be sold by negotiation, then the reasons therefore shall be included in the resolution.

*(Amended 7–2–1979 by O–12687 N.S.)*

(7) The construction manager at risk contract has provisions that prohibit any construction services from being performed until all of the following are satisfied:

(A) The potential environmental impacts of the project have been considered as required by the California Environmental Quality Act;

(B) The City and the construction manager at risk entity have agreed upon a guaranteed maximum price;

(C) An amendment to the contract, or a separate contract, authorizing the construction services to proceed that includes the guaranteed maximum price has been signed by the City and the construction manager at risk entity;

(8) The construction manager at risk contract is in writing.

(b) Notwithstanding section 22.3018, the City Manager is authorized to enter into an amendment, or a separate contract as part of the construction manager at risk contract for the construction services phase provided that all of the following are met:

(1) The potential environmental impacts of the project have been considered as required by the California Environmental Quality Act;

(2) The guaranteed maximum price does not exceed the City Manager’s authority to award a major public works contract as established in section 22.3102 and the combined total amount for the preconstruction services and construction services for the project does not exceed the City Manager’s combined authority to award consultant contracts and major public works contracts included in sections 22.3207 and 22.3102, respectively;

(3) The amendment, or additional contract, does not cause the project costs to exceed the total amount allocated for the project in the Annual Capital Improvements Program budget adopted by City Council;

(4) The City Council has not required, by resolution or ordinance, that the project be returned to City Council for approval prior to amendment;

(5) The amendment to, or separate contract as part of the construction manager at risk contract, for the construction services is in writing.

